      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64659,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 320,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adenomyomas range in size from 1.0 to 13.0&nbsp;cm {25219586; 24582628}. They can be polypoid or (less frequently) intramural, well-circumscribed nodules with a greyish-white or yellow trabecular cut surface. Cystically dilated glands may be visible grossly {25219586}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:14:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64660,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 320,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Histopathology reveals a well-circumscribed nodule composed of cystically dilated or widely spaced glands forming lobules. The glands are lined by endocervical-type epithelium, but they also show tubal, endometrioid, squamous, and rarely mesonephric differentiation {8685218; 25219586}. Intraglandular papillary projections can be present. The epithelium lining the glands lacks cytological atypia; occasional mitotic figures may be present {25219586}. The stroma is fibromuscular with bundles and fascicles of bland smooth muscle. Occasional bizarre symplastic nuclei, focal gland rupture, and mucin extravasation with stromal response can be seen in rare cases {10907166; 25219586}.</p>\r\n<p>The differential diagnosis includes lobular endocervical glandular hyperplasia and minimal deviation endocervical adenocarcinoma. Lobular endocervical glandular hyperplasia is rarely polypoid and does not have notable smooth muscle stroma {17578368}. Minimal deviation endocervical adenocarcinoma presents as a poorly defined mass infiltrating the cervical wall. The differential diagnosis also includes atypical polypoid adenomyoma of the uterus.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:14:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
